ds class next we have the cow class  which will be extending the same  animal sounds pairing class next we have  cat and followed by that we have the dog  so basically what we are going to do  here is we are going to call the sound  method  and the sound method belonging to that  particular class  will be executed based on the parameter  or based on the  object that got created now let's try to  execute this program and see the output  so there you go the program was  successfully executed and the sound  method is successfully implemented based  on the  method call so you can see that we have  called the cow  sound method twice and it got mo  over here so that's what the cow makes  as a sound and dog says something  similar like that  so basically the method calls are  resolved during the  run time based on the object created and  the method called  so basically in polymorphism we also  have method overriding and method  overloading  we will understand the concepts through  an example  so as discussed before we have the same  addition operation  example over here so here we have  different types of methods they might  look the same because the name of the  method is add basically  but if you look at the parameters a  little closely you can see that the  first method has  two parameters and both the parameters  are of integer data type  and similarly when you look at to the  third  add method it also has two  parameters but the data type is double  so when you pass a double kind of  data type then the third method will be  used  if you provide the integer data types  then the first method will be used to  perform the edition operation  but if you provide four parameters  all of double type then the second  method will be used  so this type of method resolution in  runtime is called as  method overloading now let's try to  execute this program and see the output  so there you go the program was  successfully executed and as you can see  in the main program  we have already forwarded the parameters  through the function call  and the data got executed and we have  the output over here  now let's go into method overriding and  understand the concept through a  practical program  so on my screen you can see another  example based on polymorphism but this  particular program deals with  method overriding so you can see the  cargo pilot and the civilian pilot  so basically they both want to fly a  plane so to fly a plane they need to  take off from the runway  so the fly plane method will be resolved  in  run time so whichever the object is  called  or whichever the object calls the method  then that particular method will be  executed for that particular object now  here we are going to comment out the  civilian  pilot and we are going to open the cargo  pilot  so when we run this program the cargo  pilot object  could be able to execute the fly plane  method now let's try to execute this  program and see the output  so there you go the program got  successfully executed and the cargo  pilot was free to take off and he just  gave us  the message which says this is cargo  pilot  ready to take off similarly when we  commit out  the cargo pilot  and try to open the civilian pilot  then things get a little changed so  this time the civilian pilot will be  executed  so here the fly plane method got  overridden by the civilian pilot  so this is how the method overriding  works in real time  now with this let's move ahead into the  next concept in java's programming  tutorial  so the next concept in java's  programming tutorial is the java  collections  so java collections are basically the  data structures used in java  so we have the java collections from the  iterable library class in  the java's library and followed by that  we have the collections  then inside the collection we have three  different varieties which are the list  queue and set so when we dig deep into  list q and sets we have the array list  linked list vector stack an